  "body": "The maintainer reported this in chat on 2026-11-22, in his own words:\n\n> bug; performance; P2; the stock simulation; is extremly slow. This was supposed to be calculated on the GPU as a shader, it doesnt seem to be the case..\n\n## What the repository shows\n\nThe maintainer is right: the stock removal does not run on the GPU. The plan exists. It is built only in part.\n\n- #2607 (research, closed) decided: \"The GPU does the display playback cut. The CPU f64 engines keep each verdict. The home for GPU compute code is `camcraft-gpu` (#2620).\" It made the slices S1 to S6.\n- S1 #2626 (the CPU f32 twin of the cell cut kernel) is merged. It is a CPU twin, by design. It runs no shader.\n- #2620 (the `camcraft-gpu` compute crate: probe, cached device, CPU fallback) is NOT merged. Its work is held on the pushed branch `issue/2620-gpu-compute-seam` at `90c98be93`, since 2026-11-19 (the gate stopped for disk space). Its label is `claimed`, P4.\n- S2 #2627 (the WGSL removal kernel for ball and flat tools, linear moves), S3 #2629 (viewport playback on the GPU, with CPU fallback), S4 #2628, S5 #2630 and S6 #2631 are open at P4. S3 to S6 are `blocked`.\n- So the playback in the viewport still uses the CPU engines. A long program is slow.\n\n## What the maintainer needs\n\nThe Simulate view plays a program at a usable speed, with a visible stock cut. The frame target is under 10 ms for a still frame (`docs/decisions/2238-frame-cost-rule.md`).\n\n## Plan\n\n1. Measure first. A scout and a worker time the playback on a real program (the `fig8` 2D Offset, EM-0800-4FL, and one 3D Pocket). The numbers go in a comment: cells, moves, milliseconds for each frame and for the cut. This shows where the time goes: the cut, the mesh, the upload, or the frame.\n2. Quick wins on the CPU path that the measure shows, if any. Each has a counter test (AGENTS.md \"Frame cost\").\n3. Finish the GPU path in order: #2620, then #2627 (S2), then #2629 (S3). Raise their priority to P2 and the type to bug (they are the fix of this issue). S4, S5 and S6 stay P4 and follow.\n4. The CPU f64 engines keep each verdict (the verify check, the collision check, the post gate). The GPU result is display only. A machine without a usable GPU falls back to the CPU path and says so.\n\n## Safety\n\nThe simulation that blocks the post is not changed by this work. Any change to the f64 engines is under the safety floor.\n\n## Done means\n\n- A recorded measure before and after, in a comment.\n- On the reference machine, the Simulate view of a `fig8` program plays at a usable frame rate.\n- A counter test: a still frame cuts nothing again.\n- A CPU fallback test for a machine without a GPU.\n",
